From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ArinUmut <arinumutg@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 18:36:05 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] mm: add a global page cache limit with async and sync reclaim

In a WSL2 guest every page the kernel touches stays committed in the
host's vmmem process, so a page cache that grows to most of the VM size
turns into memory pressure on the Windows host.  Add a limit on the file
LRUs that is independent of the total memory size.

Two limits, both off by default and usable alone or together:

  soft_limit  kpclimitd reclaims file pages in the background once the
              file LRUs grow past the limit, down to a little below it.
  hard_limit  while clean page cache is above the limit, a task adding
              folios to the page cache first reclaims about as many file
              pages as it adds (the memory.max model, file pages only).

The hook sits at the top of filemap_add_folio(), where the folio is not
yet locked or visible and memcg already reclaims for memory.max with the
same gfp mask.  It is a static branch, so it costs a patched-out jump
while no limit is set; when enabled it reads the global counters only
every 64 pages per CPU.

Reclaim is try_to_free_mem_cgroup_pages() on root_mem_cgroup without
MEMCG_RECLAIM_MAY_SWAP (get_scan_count() then scans file LRUs only) and
with MEMCG_RECLAIM_PROACTIVE (no vmpressure side effects), wrapped in
psi_memstall and fs_reclaim annotations like page allocator reclaim.

The hard limit is best effort and never fails an allocation:
 - dirty and writeback pages are excluded (direct reclaim cannot write
   them back);
 - non-blocking, NOFAIL, NOIO, PF_MEMALLOC, kthread, dying-task and
   dropbehind insertions skip it and only wake the worker;
 - reclaim is skipped on cgroup v1 (no writeback throttling there);
 - inserters only reclaim what they add, and writeback turns dirty
   pages clean without any insertion, so while the file LRUs (dirty
   pages included) are above the hard limit the worker keeps watching:
   it re-checks every HZ/4, and inserters that see clean excess still
   wake it at once;
 - a call that reclaims less than half its target arms a 20 ms back-off
   that doubles up to 1 s, so unreclaimable cache is not rescanned on
   every insertion.  The worker backs off the same way (HZ/4 to 8 s).

Interface: built-in module parameters pagecache_limit.soft_limit= and
pagecache_limit.hard_limit= (bytes, K/M/G/T suffix, 0 = off, >= 64M
otherwise) on the command line and under
/sys/module/pagecache_limit/parameters/, plus a read-only "stats" file.
Command line values are stored and applied by a late initcall, once the
memcg root exists.
